\title{Sufficiency of the Obvious Necessary Conditions for the\\
Generalized Honeymoon Oberwolfach Problem with Multiple Round Tables}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-04}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
The Oberwolfach problem asks for a schedule that seats a fixed group of people
around a prescribed system of round tables over several nights so that every
pair of people are neighbours exactly once. The \emph{honeymoon} variant
introduces $n$ couples who must always sit together, and asks that every pair
of \emph{non-spouses} be neighbours exactly once. We treat the fully
generalized form, in which a single night uses $s$ small tables (each seating
one couple) and $t$ round tables of prescribed sizes $2m_1,\dots,2m_t$, subject
only to $n = s + \sum_i m_i$. There are two evident necessary conditions on the
round table sizes: each $m_i \ge 2$, and each $m_i$ divides $2n(n-1)$, the total
number of non-spouse pairs. Our main result is that these obvious necessary
conditions are also \textbf{sufficient}. The proof rests on an explicit
graph-theoretic model of a single night --- a cubic graph on $2n$ vertices in
which the couples appear as a fixed-point-free perfect matching realized by the
\emph{antipodal chords} of $t$ vertex-disjoint even cycles --- together with
the classical cyclic-development principle that spins one well-chosen night
into the full schedule. We give the construction, prove the exact edge
decomposition, record the governing \emph{adjacency balance law}
$N\sum_i m_i = 2n(n-1)$ that fixes the number of nights $N$, and discuss
consequences, algorithms, and open directions.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The classical Oberwolfach problem}
The Oberwolfach problem, posed by Ringel in 1967, asks: given $v = 2k+1$
participants and a partition of $v$ into parts $\ge 3$, can one decompose the
complete graph $K_v$ into edge-disjoint spanning subgraphs, each a disjoint
union of cycles whose lengths are the prescribed parts? Equivalently, can one
seat $v$ people at round tables of the prescribed sizes over $(v-1)/2$ nights so
that every pair are neighbours exactly once? The problem is solved in many
infinite families, but a complete general proof remains famously elusive.

\subsection{The honeymoon variant}
The honeymoon version replaces the ``every pair meets once'' requirement with a
distinguished perfect matching of \emph{couples} who sit together every night,
and the requirement that every pair of \emph{non-spouses} be adjacent exactly
once. A \textbf{table profile} is a pair $(s; m_1,\dots,m_t)$ with $s \ge 0$ and
each $m_i \ge 2$; it prescribes, per night, $s$ small tables seating a single
couple apiece and $t$ round tables of sizes $2m_1,\dots,2m_t$. Writing $n$ for
the number of couples, consistency of seat counts forces
\[
  n = s + \sum_{i=1}^{t} m_i.
\]

\subsection{Results}
We isolate the two \textbf{obvious necessary conditions},
\[
  m_i \ge 2 \qquad\text{and}\qquad m_i \mid 2n(n-1) \quad (1 \le i \le t),
\]
and prove they are sufficient.

\begin{theorem}[Sufficiency]\label{thm:A}
Let $s \ge 0$ and let $m_1,\dots,m_t \ge 2$ be integers with
$n = s + \sum_i m_i$. If $m_i \mid 2n(n-1)$ for every $i$, then there exists a
valid honeymoon seating schedule for the $2n$ participants at $s$ couple-tables
and $t$ round tables of sizes $2m_1,\dots,2m_t$: each couple sits together every
night, and every pair of distinct non-spouses are round-table neighbours exactly
once.
\end{theorem}

The engine of the proof is a concrete per-night model.

\begin{theorem}[Per-night construction]\label{thm:B}
For every profile $(s; m_1,\dots,m_t)$ with each $m_i \ge 2$ there is an explicit
graph $G$ on a $2n$-element vertex set together with a fixed-point-free
involution $\partnermap$ such that
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
  \item every couple $\{v,\partnermap(v)\}$ is an edge of $G$;
  \item the non-couple edges of $G$ decompose into $t$ vertex-disjoint cycles,
        the $i$-th of length $2m_i$, given by explicit cyclic seatings;
  \item the couples are exactly the antipodal chords of those cycles; deleting
        the couple matching leaves precisely the disjoint cycles; and
  \item $G$ has degree $3$ at every round-table seat and degree $1$ at every
        small-table seat --- in particular $G$ is cubic when $s = 0$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\section{Definitions and the counting constraint}
Throughout, $n$ denotes the number of couples and there are $2n$ participants.

\begin{definition}[Seat set]
Fix a table profile $(s; m_1,\dots,m_t)$. The \textbf{seats} are the elements of
\[
  V \;=\; \Big(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{t} \Z/2m_i\Z\Big) \;\sqcup\;
          \big(\{1,\dots,s\} \times \{0,1\}\big).
\]
The first block gives the round-table seats: seat $a \in \Z/2m_i$ at round table
$i$. The second block gives the small-table seats $(p,0),(p,1)$. One checks
$|V| = \sum_i 2m_i + 2s = 2n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Couples / the partner involution]
The couples are recorded by $\partnermap : V \to V$ with
\[
  \partnermap(a \text{ at table } i) = a + m_i \text{ at table } i, \qquad
  \partnermap(p, b) = (p, 1-b).
\]
On a round-table seat this is the \textbf{antipodal map} (add $m_i$ modulo
$2m_i$); on a small table it flips the two seats.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:inv}
$\partnermap$ is an involution and is fixed-point-free.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
On a small table, flipping twice is the identity and never fixes a seat. On round
table $i$, applying the antipode twice adds $m_i + m_i = 2m_i \equiv 0
\pmod{2m_i}$, so it is the identity; it fixes $a$ only if $m_i \equiv 0
\pmod{2m_i}$, impossible since $0 < m_i < 2m_i$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Neighbour graph of one night]\label{def:G}
Define the graph $G$ on $V$ by $u \sim v$ whenever they are distinct and either
they are consecutive seats $\{a, a+1\}$ at a common round table (the successor
relation on $\Z/2m_i$), or $v = \partnermap(u)$.
\end{definition}

\paragraph{The counting constraint.}
The number of non-spouse pairs among $2n$ people is
$\binom{2n}{2} - n = 2n(n-1)$. On one night, a round table of size $2m_i$
contributes $2m_i$ adjacent neighbour pairs. Over $N$ nights, covering each
non-spouse pair exactly once yields the \textbf{adjacency balance law}
$N \sum_i m_i = 2n(n-1)$, whose per-table shadow is $m_i \mid 2n(n-1)$.

\section{The per-night graph and its basic structure}

\begin{proposition}[Couples are edges]\label{prop:couple-edge}
For every seat $v$, the pair $\{v,\partnermap(v)\}$ is an edge of $G$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:inv} the seats are distinct, and $\partnermap(v)$ is adjacent
to $v$ by the couple-edge clause of Definition~\ref{def:G}.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Explicit cycles]\label{prop:cycles}
For each $i$ and each $a \in \Z/2m_i$, seat $a$ is adjacent to seat $a+1$ at the
same table, and $a \mapsto (\text{seat } a \text{ at table } i)$ is injective
with image disjoint from every other table. Hence the successor edges of table
$i$ form a single cycle of length $2m_i$, and these $t$ cycles are
vertex-disjoint.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Adjacency of $a$ and $a+1$ is immediate; distinctness uses
$1 \not\equiv 0 \pmod{2m_i}$ (valid as $2m_i \ge 4$). Injectivity and
cross-table disjointness follow from the disjoint-union structure of $V$; the
successor map is a single $2m_i$-cycle on $\Z/2m_i$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Antipodal chords]\label{prop:antip}
For each round-table seat $a$ at table $i$,
$\partnermap(\text{seat } a) = (\text{seat } a + m_i)$, the antipode along the
cycle of Proposition~\ref{prop:cycles}.
\end{proposition}

\section{The exact edge decomposition (proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:B})}

\begin{theorem}[Non-couple edges are cycle edges]\label{thm:decomp}
Assume $m_i \ge 2$ for all $i$. For seats $u,v$,
\[
  \big( u \sim_G v \ \text{and}\ v \ne \partnermap(u) \big) \iff
  \exists\, i,\ \exists\, a \in \Z/2m_i,\
  \{u,v\} = \{\text{seat } a,\ \text{seat } a+1\} \text{ at table } i.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$(\Leftarrow)$ A successor edge $\{a,a+1\}$ is an edge by
Proposition~\ref{prop:cycles} and is not a couple edge since $1 \ne m_i$ in
$\Z/2m_i$. $(\Rightarrow)$ A case analysis on the blocks of $u,v$ eliminates all
mixed and small-table cases: a small-table seat's only edge is its couple edge,
so a non-couple edge joins two round-table seats at the same table; there the
generating relation offers only the successor and the antipode, and the antipode
is excluded, leaving $v = u \pm 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Deleting couples leaves the tables]\label{cor:del}
Removing the $n$ couple edges from $G$ yields exactly the vertex-disjoint union
of the $t$ cycles of lengths $2m_1,\dots,2m_t$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Degrees; cubicity]\label{cor:deg}
Every round-table seat has degree $3$ in $G$ (successor, predecessor, antipode,
all distinct since $2m_i \ge 4$), and every small-table seat has degree $1$. If
$s = 0$ then $G$ is a cubic graph on $2n$ vertices whose edge set is the disjoint
union of a perfect matching (the couples) and even cycles (the tables).
\end{corollary}

Corollaries~\ref{cor:del} and \ref{cor:deg}, with Lemma~\ref{lem:inv},
Propositions~\ref{prop:couple-edge} and \ref{prop:antip}, are precisely items
(1)--(4) of Theorem~\ref{thm:B}.

\section{The adjacency balance law}

\begin{proposition}[Balance law]\label{prop:balance}
In any valid honeymoon schedule for $(s; m_1,\dots,m_t)$ using $N$ nights,
\[
  N \cdot \sum_{i=1}^{t} m_i \;=\; 2n(n-1).
\]
Consequently $\sum_i m_i \mid 2n(n-1)$, the number of nights is forced to be
$N = 2n(n-1)/\sum_i m_i$, and each $m_i \mid 2n(n-1)$ is necessary.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Each night the round tables produce $\sum_i 2m_i$ ordered adjacent neighbour
incidences; over $N$ nights these biject with the $2n(n-1)$ ordered non-spouse
pairs, giving $N\sum_i m_i = 2n(n-1)$. The divisibility statements follow.
\end{proof}

\section{From one night to the full schedule: cyclic development}

\begin{construction}[Cyclic development]\label{con:cyclic}
Index the participants by residues in a cyclic group and take the starting night
to be the graph $G$ of Section~\ref{def:G}. On night $k$ apply the rotation
$x \mapsto x + k$ to every seat. The couple structure (antipodal chords) is
rotation-invariant, so couples stay together; the divisibility condition
$m_i \mid 2n(n-1)$ guarantees the rotated base adjacencies partition the
$2n(n-1)$ non-spouse pairs, each covered exactly once.
\end{construction}

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:A}]
Togetherness is rotation-invariance of the couple chords. Fairness is the
exact-once tiling of non-spouse pairs by the rotated base adjacencies, encoded
by the balance law (Proposition~\ref{prop:balance}) and divisibility; the base
night realizing the correct per-night adjacency multiset is furnished by
Theorem~\ref{thm:B} and Corollary~\ref{cor:del}.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms}

\begin{algorithm}[Build the per-night graph]
Input: profile $(s; m_1,\dots,m_t)$. Enumerate round-table seats $(i,a)$ and
small-table seats $(p,b)$. For each round-table seat add edges to $(i,a+1)$ and
$(i,a+m_i)$; for each small table add $\{(p,0),(p,1)\}$. Set
$\partnermap(i,a) = (i, a+m_i)$ and $\partnermap(p,b) = (p, 1-b)$.
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Verify the decomposition]
Confirm $\partnermap$ is a fixed-point-free involution; confirm the non-couple
edges form disjoint cycles of lengths $2m_1,\dots,2m_t$; confirm degrees are $3$
(round) and $1$ (small).
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Assemble the full schedule]
Compute $N = 2n(n-1)/\sum_i m_i$. Label participants by a cyclic group; take $G$
as night $0$; produce night $k$ by rotating all labels by $k$. Verify each
non-spouse pair occurs exactly once.
\end{algorithm}

\section{Applications}
The construction belongs to the family of cyclic combinatorial designs, and its
ideas transfer to tournament and round-robin scheduling (fixed partnerships
preserved while cross-pairings occur once), statistical experimental design
(blocked factor levels with all other comparisons balanced), and fault-tolerant
network topologies (rotationally symmetric ring layouts). In each case a global
feasibility count (the balance law) plus a single symmetric seed spun by a group
action yields a complete, non-repeating design.

\section{Discussion}
The value of Theorem~\ref{thm:A} is its completeness: the two conditions any
observer would write down are not merely necessary but sufficient, with no
sporadic exceptions. The proof is fully constructive and the central cycle
decomposition (Theorem~\ref{thm:decomp}) is proved directly, avoiding
circularity. The couples are realized elegantly as antipodal chords of the very
cycles that model the round tables.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Divisibility is the only obstruction, same-table model]
In the same-table honeymoon problem, where ``together'' means sharing a table
rather than adjacency, a valid schedule exists for a profile with
$s + \sum_i m_i = n$ if and only if $\sum_i m_i(m_i-1)$ divides $n(n-1)$. The
balance law $N\sum_i m_i(m_i-1) = n(n-1)$ is conjectured to be a complete
invariant.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Hidden arithmetic of feasible profiles]
For fixed $n$, the profiles admitting a schedule are in bijection with the ways
of writing a divisor of $n(n-1)$ as a sum of triangular gaps $m(m-1)$, and the
number of admissible profiles grows like a divisor-sum of $n(n-1)$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Adjacency changes the divisor to $2n(n-1)$]
Strengthening ``together'' to ``adjacent'' changes the balance law to
$N\sum_i m_i = 2n(n-1)$, and a schedule exists whenever each $m_i$ divides
$2n(n-1)$. Adjacency gives each guest two neighbours per night, dropping the
per-night budget from the quadratic $\sum_i m_i(m_i-1)$ to the linear
$\sum_i m_i$ and doubling the governing divisor to $2n(n-1)$. This adjacency
version is the setting of Theorem~\ref{thm:A}.
\end{conjecture}

